{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>If you do this thing, and Elohim commands you.</b> Surely Moses would not do this thing until after HaShem would command him, and he needs to take counsel with the Almighty first. If so, he should have reversed the order and said 'and Elohim commands you' before 'if you do this thing.' It appears that this refers to what is written: 'And you shall warn them concerning the statutes and the teachings' etc. (Exodus 18:20), and afterward: 'And you shall see from among all the people' etc. (Exodus 18:21). For you must first teach them the judgments of HaShem, for it is not within everyone's power to appoint a judge who is not versed and not discerning. And concerning this he said: 'If you do this thing' — to first teach them the statutes of Elohim and His teachings, then 'and Elohim commands you' — it is possible that the Holy One, blessed be He, will agree through you, but before this He would surely not agree." "And as for what is said: 'And all this people upon its place shall come in peace.' It appears that the word 'אִישׁ' is missing here, for he should have said 'each man upon his place' etc., and its meaning would be that each individual man would sit in peace under his vine and under his fig tree, when there is justice below and peace upon Israel. And from the fact that he did not say 'אִישׁ,' infer that he is speaking of a general place singled out for all the people collectively as one — and this is none other than the land of Israel, as it is written (Deuteronomy 16:20): 'Justice, justice shall you pursue, so that you may live and possess the land.' Rashi explained: 'The appointment of qualified judges is worthy to give life to Israel and to settle them upon their land.' Therefore it does not say here 'shall sit in peace' but rather 'shall come in peace' — to say that by virtue of the appointment of qualified judges, all this people will come upon its designated place, which is the soil of Israel. There all the people will come in peace, for they have not yet come there. And this teaches that violence corrupts the land, and the generation of the Flood proves it. And they were exiled on account of the absence of justice, as it is said (Isaiah 1:23): 'The orphan they do not judge' etc. And for the future: 'Zion shall be redeemed through justice' (Isaiah 1:27). And he said to Moses 'and you will be able to endure' here, while 'also all the people upon its place shall come in peace' — he excluded Moses from the collective, for it had already been decreed upon him in the verse: 'Now you shall see what I will do to Pharaoh' etc. (Exodus 6:1), that Moses would not come there under any circumstances."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :register {:flags {0 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:16.105424707Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}